url: https://zenodo.org/records/23489459
abstract: Basaltic materials occur in cementitious systems as raw-meal constituents,
  separated chemical feedstocks, mineral powders, aggregates, fibers and composite
  reinforcement. These uses are connected by geological origin but differ in phase
  assemblage, processing history, formulation and performance. This descriptive literature
  survey organizes scientific, technical, patent and industrial sources along the
  sequence provenance → chemistry and mineral phases → processing → functional role
  → observed properties. It covers conventional clinkering, silicate separation, mechanical
  and thermal activation, Portland-cement blends, alkali-activated binders, aggregate
  concretes, dispersed fibers, textiles and polymer-bound reinforcement. Experimental
  findings include both strength gains and losses, weak and measurable reactivity,
  and durability outcomes that depend on exposure and test method. Comparisons distinguish
  substitution of cement from substitution of sand, natural rock from synthetic basalt-composition
  glass, and laboratory observations from process models, patent disclosures and producer
  statements. Cross-sectional analysis addresses rheology, hydration, interfaces,
  characterization, material and energy accounting, historical terminology and specification
  pathways. The evidence supports formulation-specific descriptions rather than a
  single performance classification for basalt. Uneven full-text recovery, unresolved
  historical citations, differences among test conditions and limited independently
  documented operating data constrain comparisons. Original-language sources and unresolved
  bibliographic discrepancies are retained without assigning unsupported precision
  or implying exhaustive multilingual coverage.
